content.thinking:
The user is showing me a CSS snippet that achieves natural backdrop-filter fade. Let me analyze it:

```css
width: 100%;
height: 100vh;
background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4);
-webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(24px);
backdrop-filter: blur(24px);
position: fixed;
top: 0;
left: 0;
z-index: 100;
will-change: opacity;
opacity: 0;


visibility: hidden;
transition: 0.32s 150ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.6, 1);
transition-property: opacity, visibility;
```

The key insight here is that only opacity and visibility are being transitioned—the backdrop-filter blur stays constant and doesn't animate. The opacity fade creates the visual effect of the entire element fading in and out, while the visibility property ensures it's completely removed from the interaction layer when hidden. The will-change hint optimizes the opacity animation performance.

The Material Design easing curve with the 150ms delay gives it that smooth, polished feel. Since backdrop-filter is applied to the element itself, it gets composited along with the opacity change—so when opacity hits zero, the blur effect disappears with it without needing its own transition. The static background-color just sits there while opacity handles all the visibility changes.

The problem with my earlier approach was trying to animate the backdrop-filter blur value itself, which caused that jarring jump. The solution is letting opacity do all the heavy lifting. The key insight is that the backdrop-filter stays constant at `blur(24px)` while only the opacity transitions — the browser composites the blur effect within the opacity layer, so the entire effect fades as a single unit rather than the blur interpolating separately. My earlier approach of transitioning from `blur(0px)` to `blur(16px)` created an unnatural effect because the blur interpolation doesn't look as smooth as a simple opacity fade.

I need to restructure the implementation: keep the backdrop-filter fixed at the target blur value, remove it from the transition list, and instead animate only opacity and visibility with the reference timing of 320ms delay and a cubic-bezier easing curve.

